{"data":{"id":76279,"api_model":"artworks","api_link":"https:\/\/api.artic.edu\/api\/v1\/artworks\/76279","is_boosted":true,"title":"Yang Pu Moving His Family","alt_titles":[],"thumbnail":{"lqip":"data:image\/gif;base64,R0lGODlhEAAFAPYAAHJgTXRgTnVkU3ZlVHlnVHpoWn5rWnxrXIJuW4VvW4FvXYtvWYVxXoVzX4lzXop0Xo14Yo54Yo54ZI55ZId4aot8bZB7ZpB6Z5J8ZZJ8Z5R9ZZN\/aZV+aJR\/apR\","width":13604,"height":4080,"alt_text":"Long scroll ink painting depicting people with their animals and belongings crossing a river."},"main_reference_number":"1952.9","has_not_been_viewed_much":false,"boost_rank":null,"date_start":1279,"date_end":1368,"date_display":"Yuan dynasty (1279\u20131368) \u5143\u671d","date_qualifier_title":null,"date_qualifier_id":null,"artist_display":"Artist Unknown\nChinese","place_of_origin":"China","description":"<p>This detail of a scroll painting depicts a village scholar (at left ) bidding farewell to his friends while transporting his family across a narrow river. Distinguished by his official government cap with its long streamers, he is accompanied by servants who valiantly attempt to carry his children and the family\u2019s belongings, which include scrolls, animals, and dishes. The rustic figure shown here may represent Yang Pu, a character who appears in twelfth- and thirteenth-century stories. As described in this folklore, Yang initially declined, and then reluctantly accepted, his appointment to a government position in the capital. In an attempt to prevent sectionalism and corruption, Chinese law forbade civil officials from working in their native districts, and many were required to move to distant cities. Given China\u2019s vastness, it was unlikely that these men, once transferred, would ever see their friends again. Such partings were frequent and often somber occasions and a favorite theme of poets and painters. The twigs that protrude from the official caps of the men depicted here may allude to the ancient Chinese custom of presenting departing friends with small branches from a willow tree. In this painting, however, the \u201cnoble parting\u201d theme has been reinterpreted with a lively combination of realism and caricature.<\/p>\n","short_description":null,"dimensions":"52.7 \u00d7 231.1 cm (20 3\/4 \u00d7 91 in.)","dimensions_detail":[{"depth":null,"width":231,"height":52,"diameter":null,"clarification":null}],"medium_display":"Handscroll; ink and light color on paper","inscriptions":null,"credit_line":"Kate S. Buckingham Endowment","catalogue_display":null,"publication_history":null,"exhibition_history":null,"provenance_text":null,"edition":null,"publishing_verification_level":"Web Basic","internal_department_id":8,"fiscal_year":1952,"fiscal_year_deaccession":null,"is_public_domain":true,"is_zoomable":true,"max_zoom_window_size":-1,"copyright_notice":null,"has_multimedia_resources":false,"has_educational_resources":false,"has_advanced_imaging":false,"colorfulness":22.406,"color":{"h":211,"l":83,"s":12,"percentage":0.06040629801315806,"population":138},"latitude":null,"longitude":null,"latlon":null,"is_on_view":false,"on_loan_display":null,"gallery_title":null,"gallery_id":null,"nomisma_id":null,"artwork_type_title":"Painting","artwork_type_id":1,"department_title":"Arts of Asia","department_id":"PC-7","artist_id":null,"artist_title":null,"alt_artist_ids":[],"artist_ids":[],"artist_titles":[],"category_ids":["PC-831","PC-7","PC-837"],"category_titles":["Essentials","Arts of Asia","Arts of Asia 100"],"term_titles":["paper (fiber product)","handscroll","folklore","painting (image making)","Chinese (culture or style)","ink","painting","asian art","children","animals","boats","rivers","trees","scholars","families","landscapes","Chinese","y\u00fcan"],"style_id":"TM-4303","style_title":"Chinese (culture or style)","alt_style_ids":["TM-5084"],"style_ids":["TM-4303","TM-5084"],"style_titles":["Chinese (culture or style)","y\u00fcan"],"classification_id":"TM-468","classification_title":"handscroll","alt_classification_i